<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="3a3db0b">&lt;p&gt;
    Before creating a workplan, it makes sense to start with the end in mind. What are we trying to achieve? What does it
    look like? By then progressively analyzing and defining what the component parts are of this objective, we will
    ultimately have a list of all the key activities required to produce this output. This technique is called WBS or work
    breakdown structure. There are two approaches to constructing a WBS; Top-Down and Bottom-Up.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Top-Down:&lt;/strong&gt; The top-down approach is typically used for projects where the project team has a good
        (intuitive) idea of the work beforehand. It begins at the goal level and successively partitions work down to lower
        levels of definition until the participants are satisfied that the work has been sufficiently defined.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Bottom- Up:&lt;/strong&gt; Bottom-up approach is more like a brainstorming session than an organized way of
        building the WBS. This approach encourages people to generate any and all activities that may have to be completed
        as part of the project and then group them into categories. The bottom-up estimate is based on the idea of
        estimating the effort required for small activities and tasks, and aggregating these numbers to form the work
        effort estimate for the whole project.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Brainstorm all activities that you think are necessary for your project.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Group activities into a few major categories with common characteristics and eliminate any activities you do not
        have to perform.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Cross-check using the top-down approach to identify any additional activities that you overlooked in the categories
        you created.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Continue in this manner until all project work is described clearly and completely.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
